          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"cofield-nikkis-touch-fc43fce909273865\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":3,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"The highlighted second “I love you,” intensified by “so much, Nikki,” directly avows Franco’s love. Because it immediately follows Nikki’s acceptance of his proposal and is explicitly delivered with a voice “full of promise,” it also helps affirm their newly undertaken marital commitment. The emotional and intimate delivery does not independently establish expressive/reflexive force.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The strength of P is somewhat debatable: the utterance is promise-inflected by its engagement context, but it does not itself explicitly state an obligation or future action.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Franco directly says the highlighted “I love you” to Nikki during their embrace immediately after she accepts his marriage proposal.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Franco repeats his declaration of love after Nikki accepts his proposal, while embracing and kissing her and before placing the engagement ring on her finger. The narration’s description of his voice as “full of promise,” together with the accepted proposal and subsequent future-oriented assurances, gives the avowal substantial commitment-renewing force. Its murmured, passionate delivery supplies emotional intensity but no positive evidence that the words are a reflex-like discharge rather than an avowal and relational affirmation.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I love you so much, Nikki.”\",\"supports\":\"The direct, intensified declaration strongly supports truth-conditional avowal of a loving state.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I love you,” he murmured against her lips, his voice full of promise.\",\"supports\":\"The narrator explicitly associates the repeated love declaration with promise-like force, supporting P while identifying the intimate delivery as contextual intensity.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately beforehand Franco proposes marriage and Nikki answers, “Yes, I will.”\",\"supports\":\"The accepted proposal situates the highlighted declaration within a newly formed marital undertaking, strengthening its performative interpretation.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Franco then slips the ring onto Nikki’s finger and later promises that they have a shared future ahead.\",\"supports\":\"The surrounding enactment and explicit future commitment corroborate the promise-inflected reading without making the love words themselves purely performative.\",\"confidence\":0.95},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is marked as an affirmative present-tense explicit “I love you” form and retained as a valid match.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms identification of the highlighted core utterance as a literal affirmative love declaration.\",\"confidence\":1.0}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T and P naturally represent the utterance as both a sincere-presented avowal and a promise-inflected affirmation of the couple’s engagement. Emotional delivery is adequately handled as context and does not require E or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The classification targets the metadata-located second occurrence within “I love you so much, Nikki,” rather than the immediately preceding repeated declaration.\"}"
        }
      ],
